\fs96 \cf2 Introduction to layouts\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/kernels/layout/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 layout
\fs32 \cb1  package}} provides a number of APIs for working with dense multidimensional arrays, which simplify writing algorithms for handling linear algebra.\
This package includes the following main types:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/kernels/layout/layout/Layout/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Layout}} struct describes an arrangement of data in memory. A layout is a function that maps a set of logical coordinates (like (x, y) in a two-dimensional array) to a linear index value. Layouts can be hierarchical (for example, representing a 2D matrix that's further subdivided into tiles).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/kernels/layout/layout_tensor/LayoutTensor/"}}{\fldrslt \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul LayoutTensor}}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
 is a flexible tensor type that combines a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Layout
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and a pointer to data.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/kernels/layout/int_tuple/IntTuple/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 IntTuple}} struct is a hierarchical tuple type, where each element of the tuple can either be an integral value or a nested 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 IntTuple
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 IntTuple
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type is used extensively for defining and indexing layouts and layout tensors.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 Example code\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa127\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb13 You can find most of the code examples on this page in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/modular/modular/tree/modular/v26.2/mojo/examples/layouts"}}{\fldrslt \ul public GitHub repo}}.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb13 Some of the concepts presented here can be a little hard to grasp from static examples, so we recommend downloading the example code and experimenting.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 What's a Layout?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A layout is a function that maps a set of logical coordinates to a single linear index value.\
For example, a layout could describe a 2x4 row-major matrix, or a 6x6 column-major matrix.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 from\cf15  layout \cf5 import\cf15  Layout\cf5 ,\cf15  print_layout\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf15  l2x4row_major \cf5 =\cf15  Layout\cf5 .\cf15 row_major\cf5 (\cf16 2\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf16 4\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf15  l6x6col_major \cf5 =\cf15  Layout\cf5 .\cf15 col_major\cf5 (\cf16 6\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf16 6\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Layouts are made up of two tuples: shape and stride, where shape describes the logical coordinate space and the stride determines the mapping to the linear index value. A layout can be written as (shape:stride). For example, a contiguous vector of length 4 can be represented as (4:1):\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic unknown.png \width6980 \height4380 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs27\fsmilli13600 \cf2 Figure 1. 1D layout (4:1)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A 3x4 row-major layout can be represented as ((3, 4):(4, 1)). That is, the shape is 3x4 and the strides are 4 and 1. You can break this down into two sub-layouts or modes: a row mode and a column mode: 3 rows with a stride of 4 (3:4, the first numbers from each tuple) and 4 columns with a stride of 1 (4:1, the second numbers from each tuple).\
The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/kernels/layout/layout/print_layout/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 print_layout()}} function generates an ASCII diagram of any 2D layout, showing the coordinates on the outside and the corresponding index values in the grid.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf15  l3x4row_major \cf5 =\cf15  Layout\cf5 .\cf15 row_major\cf5 (\cf16 3\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf16 4\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14 print_layout\cf5 (\cf15 l3x4row_major\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Output:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf15 \cb14 ((3, 4):(4, 1))\cb1 \
\cb14        0    1    2    3\cb1 \
\cb14     +----+----+----+----+\cb1 \
\cb14  0  |  0 |  1 |  2 |  3 |\cb1 \
\cb14     +----+----+----+----+\cb1 \
\cb14  1  |  4 |  5 |  6 |  7 |\cb1 \
\cb14     +----+----+----+----+\cb1 \
\cb14  2  |  8 |  9 | 10 | 11 |\cb1 \
\cb14     +----+----+----+----+\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The coordinate to index mapping is performed by calculating the dot product of the logical coordinates and the corresponding strides. For example, given the coordinates (i, j) and the layout shown above, the index value is
\fs38\fsmilli19360 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf2 i\
\uc0\u8727 \
4\
+\
j\
\uc0\u8727 \
1\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf2 i*4 + j*1\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0 \cf2 i
\f4 \uc0\u8727 
\f0 4+j
\f4 \uc0\u8727 
\f0 1
\fs32 . So coordinate (1, 1) maps to 5, as shown in the diagram.\
The following example shows how to use a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Layout
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to convert between coordinates and index values.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf15  coords \cf5 =\cf15  IntTuple\cf5 (\cf16 1\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf16 1\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf15  idx \cf5 =\cf15  l3x4row_major\cf5 (\cf15 coords\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf19 "index at coordinates (1, 1): "\cf5 ,\cf15  idx\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf19 "coordinates at index 7:"\cf5 ,\cf15  l3x4row_major\cf5 .\cf15 idx2crd\cf5 (\cf16 7\cf5 ))\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Output:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf15 \cb14 index at coordinates (1, 1):  5\cb1 \
\cb14 coordinates at index 7: (1, 3)\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 As this example shows, the layout is a function that takes a set of integer coordinates and returns a single integer (the linear index). The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Layout
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct also provides an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/kernels/layout/layout/Layout/#idx2crd"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 idx2crd()}} method that transforms a linear index into a set of logical coordinates.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 Printing layouts\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb13 You can use 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 print_layout()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  to print a diagram of any 2D layout. You can pass any layout to the built-in 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 print()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  function to print a string representation of the layout in the form of a (shape:stride) pair.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 IntTuple: representing hierarchical shapes and strides\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A layout's shape and stride are represented using the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/kernels/layout/int_tuple/IntTuple/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 IntTuple}} type. Each element of an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 IntTuple
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is either an integer value or a nested 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 IntTuple
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . You can create nested 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 IntTuples
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 IntTuple
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  constructor:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf15  shape1 \cf5 =\cf15  IntTuple\cf5 (\cf16 4\cf5 ,\cf15  IntTuple\cf5 (\cf16 2\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf16 2\cf5 ))\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 A layout's shape and stride tuples must be congruent\'97that is, they need to have the same hierarchical structure: the tuples must have the same number of elements, and any elements that are nested tuples must also have the same number of elements.\
The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/kernels/layout/int_tuple/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 int_tuple}} package provides a number of functions for working with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 IntTuple
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . For example, it provides a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/kernels/layout/int_tuple/congruent/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 congruent()}} function for testing the congruency of two tuples.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Modes\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A layout has one or more modes, where a mode is a shape:stride pair. For example, the 1D vector layout (8:1) has a single mode: 8 elements with a stride of 1:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs27\fsmilli13600 \cf2 Figure 2. 1D layout\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The 2D row-major matrix layout ((2, 4):(4, 1)) has two modes, 2:4 (the first numbers from each tuple) and 4:1 (the second numbers from each tuple). Taking them right to left, the second mode describes 4 columns with a stride of one. The first mode specifies that there are two of these groups with a stride of 4:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs27\fsmilli13600 \cf2 Figure 3. 2D layout with strides\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In a column-major layout, the row number varies the fastest, so a column-major 2x4 matrix has the layout ((2, 4):(1, 2)) and looks like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs27\fsmilli13600 \cf2 Figure 4. 2D column-major layout with strides\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A layout's rank is the number of modes in its shape. A rank-1 (or 1D) layout describes a vector. A rank-2 layout describes a 2D matrix, and so on.\
A layout's size is defined as the product of all of the modes in the layout's shape. To put it another way, it's the number of elements that the layout addresses: that is, the domain of the layout function.\
Modes can also be nested to represent more complicated strides along a dimension. For example, the layout (8:1) represents a 1D vector of 8 elements.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs27\fsmilli13600 \cf2 Figure 5. 1D vector layout\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The layout (((4, 2):(1, 4))) is also a 1D vector of 8 elements. The extra set of parentheses indicates a nested or hierarchical mode. Instead of being represented by a single mode like 8:1, this layout's single dimension is represented by the multi-mode (4, 2):(1, 4):\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs27\fsmilli13600 \cf2 Figure 6. 1D layout with nested modes\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Note that in the nested modes, there's no notion of row and column. You can think of the first mode as the "inner" mode (defining a group) and the next mode as an "outer" mode (defining a repeat of the group) as shown above.\
A set of nested modes (a multi-mode) counts as a single mode when considering the parent layout's rank. For example, the layouts (8:1) and (((4, 2):(1, 4))) are both rank-1 layouts.\
This gets more interesting when we move to two dimensions. Consider the following 2D layouts:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs27\fsmilli13600 \cf2 Figure 7. Two 2D layouts\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Layouts A and B are both 2D matrix layouts with the same overall 2D shape, but with the elements in a different order. Layout B is tiled, so instead of being in row-major or column-major order, four consecutive indices are grouped into each 2x2 tile. This is sometimes called tile-major order.\
We can break this tiled layout into two modes, one for the rows and one for the columns:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Layout B has a row mode of (2, 2):(1, 4). We can further break this into two sub-modes: the inner mode, 2:1, defines a group of two rows with a stride of one. The outer mode, 2:4, specifies that the group occurs twice with a stride of 4.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The column has the mode (2, 2):(2, 8). Once again we can break this into two sub-modes: (2:2) defines a group of two columns with a stride of two, and the group occurs twice with a stride of 8 (2:8).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 If all of those modes are swimming before your eyes, take a moment to study the figure and trace out the strides yourself.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Coordinates\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Coordinates for layouts can be written in the same format as the shape tuple. For example, coordinates for layout B above can be written ((i, j), (k, l)). However, this layout can also be addressed as a logical 2D matrix, just like layout A. So ((0, 1), (0, 1)) and (2, 2) are both valid coordinates that map to the same index.\
In fact, this is true for any layout: the layout can be addressed with 1D or 2D coordinates as well as its "natural" coordinates. When mapping coordinates, the dimensions are traversed in colexicographical order (that is, a generalized column-major order, where the leftmost coordinate varies fastest). Table 1 shows how different 1D and 2D coordinates map to the "natural" coordinates of the ((2, 2), (2, 2)) shape shown above:\

